WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:15.570
- Hi, I'm Bench Ansfeld,
and I'm graduating

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:19.530
from the Department of American
Studies with my doctorate.

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:22.494
I'm a historian of race
and capitalism with a focus

00:00:22.494 --> 00:00:26.510
on US cities and cultural
studies of the built environment.

00:00:26.510 --> 00:00:30.487
And I'm a 2021 winner of the
Field Prize for my dissertation

00:00:30.487 --> 00:00:33.690
"Born in Flames: Arson, Racial Capitalism,

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:35.140
and the Reinsuring of the Bronx

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:36.853
in the Late Twentieth Century"

00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:40.330
So Born in Flames examines
the wave of landlord arson

00:00:40.330 --> 00:00:41.620
that coursed through the Bronx

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:45.150
and scores of other US
cities in the 1970s.

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:48.340
Though commonly confused with
the uprisings of the sixties,

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:51.670
these fires were lit, not
for protests, but for profit,

00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:53.870
most of which float into
the ironically named

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:57.450
fire industries, that's finance,
insurance, and real estate.

00:00:57.450 --> 00:01:00.850
It's by asking why cities went
up in flames in these years,

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:02.430
how the fires were extinguished,

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:04.550
and what arose in their ashes,

00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:06.010
the project casts new light

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:10.610
on the restructuring of US
cities over the past five decades

00:01:10.610 --> 00:01:14.050
and track how the turn
towards financialization

00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:15.950
reached its peak right in the aftermath

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:17.850
of the civil rights movement's heyday

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:20.020
and asks what the coinciding nature

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:22.280
of those two formations can tell us

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:26.900
about this moment in the
history of race and capitalism.

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:30.870
At the center is the study
of a state subsidized

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:33.120
fire insurance program that was initiated

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:36.303
as a direct response to the
uprisings of the sixties.

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:39.880
Ostensibly intended to
end insurance redlining,

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:43.530
the program was more of a bailout
for the insurance industry

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:45.710
and it inadvertently
incentivized landlords

00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:48.360
to set their buildings on fire.

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:51.650
Through a study of the Bronx,
known as the arson capital

00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:53.890
of the world in these years,

00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:56.170
I chronicle how Black and Latin X tenants

00:01:56.170 --> 00:01:58.870
mobilized dozens of
anti arson organizations

00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:02.890
that by the early 1980s stem
the tide of the burnings.

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:04.820
And then a final thread of
the project turns an eye

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:07.150
to the cultural responses to the buyers

00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:10.810
by considering how the
ubiquity of the fire trope

00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:13.750
in films, fiction, and popular music

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:16.470
of the era from "The Towering Inferno"

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:20.570
to "Disco Inferno" offers
a hidden transcript

00:02:20.570 --> 00:02:24.140
for contested interpretations
of the arson wave.

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:26.560
These various threads
weave together a story

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:29.760
about risk, real estate,
and power in an era

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:34.150
with race and race thinking
were undergoing profound shifts.

00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:34.983
Thanks.

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:38.490
- Hi, my name is Stefano Daniele

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:41.740
and I graduated in December, 2020

00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:44.493
with a PhD from the
Department of Neuroscience.

00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:48.510
My work focuses on global brain ischemia,

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:51.060
or in other words, how the brain responds

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:52.520
to interruptions in blood flow

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:55.460
such as in the case of cardiac arrest.

00:02:55.460 --> 00:02:58.370
I'm a 2021 Porter Prize winner,

00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:00.417
And my submission is entitled

00:03:00.417 --> 00:03:02.680
"Ex Vivo Normothermic Restoration

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:04.840
of Circulation and Cellular Functions

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:07.757
in the Large Mammalian
Brain Hours Postmortem."

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:11.910
Now to place my PhD work in context,

00:03:11.910 --> 00:03:14.400
traditional understanding
maintains that the brain

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:17.721
is highly vulnerable to
interruptions in blood flow,

00:03:17.721 --> 00:03:19.960
tolerating only several minutes

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:24.070
of circulatory arrest
before undergoing widespread

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:26.883
and irreversible loss
of cellular viability.

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:29.960
However, over the past century,

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:33.130
multiple lines of observation have brought

00:03:33.130 --> 00:03:35.890
into question the finality
of cellular viability

00:03:35.890 --> 00:03:38.750
minutes or even hours after death

00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:40.930
in the large mammalian brain.

00:03:40.930 --> 00:03:44.900
Therefore, the central
question I asked in my PhD work

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:49.460
was how long after prolonged
global ischemia do cells

00:03:49.460 --> 00:03:53.180
within the large mammalian
brain remain viable.

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:55.780
Now to test this question, my colleagues

00:03:55.780 --> 00:03:59.200
and I developed a novel
technology called brain X

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:01.990
which consists of three components,

00:04:01.990 --> 00:04:05.150
the first of which is a circulatory device

00:04:05.150 --> 00:04:07.100
that is comprised of a variety

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:09.379
of membranes, pumps, and tubes

00:04:09.379 --> 00:04:14.270
that mechanically mimic many
of the functions of the heart,

00:04:14.270 --> 00:04:16.320
the lungs, and the kidney.

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:18.900
The second is a uniquely formulated

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:23.900
blood analog solution that is
entirely synthetic, acellular,

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:26.110
and it's supplemented with a cocktail

00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:30.080
of protective agents to prevent cell death

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:34.800
and promote recovery from
prolonged periods of ischemia.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:37.300
And the third is a surgical procedure

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:39.480
to isolate the brain from the skull,

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:43.350
allowing us to connect it
to the circulatory device.

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:47.180
Now utilizing this system,
my colleagues and I observed

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:51.260
that circulation and cellular
functions can be restored

00:04:51.260 --> 00:04:55.990
in the large mammalian brain
up to four hours after death.

00:04:55.990 --> 00:04:59.500
Now, these findings indicate that cells

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:03.800
in the large mammalian brain
retain a greater resilience

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:08.050
to lack of blood flow
than was once appreciated.

00:05:08.050 --> 00:05:12.290
And importantly, these findings
may help lay the groundwork

00:05:12.290 --> 00:05:14.790
for the development of novel therapies

00:05:14.790 --> 00:05:17.180
to salvage brain tissue function

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:20.580
in patients suffering from
devastating conditions

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:23.393
such as cardiac arrest and stroke.

00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:26.250
I thank you so much for your time,

00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:29.850
and I thank the Porter Prize
Committee for this honor,

00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:31.769
and I continue wishing you health

00:05:31.769 --> 00:05:34.871
and safety during this difficult period.

00:05:34.871 --> 00:05:36.223
Thank you again.

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:39.640
- My name is Zuri Sullivan.

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:44.640
and I earned my PhD in
immunobiology in December, 2020.

00:05:44.790 --> 00:05:47.120
I'm fascinated by how the immune system

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:49.743
helps animals adapt to
different environments.

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:53.490
I am a 2021 winner of the Porter Prize,

00:05:53.490 --> 00:05:57.330
and my prize submission was
entitled "Food Quality Control:

00:05:57.330 --> 00:06:00.857
Nutrition and Immunity in
Intestinal Homeostasis."

00:06:02.190 --> 00:06:04.060
The gastrointestinal tract is

00:06:04.060 --> 00:06:06.860
a multi kingdom cellular ecosystem

00:06:06.860 --> 00:06:08.750
that facilitates the procurement

00:06:08.750 --> 00:06:11.540
of nutrients from the environment.

00:06:11.540 --> 00:06:14.890
It is in constant contact
with the outside world

00:06:14.890 --> 00:06:18.200
and is therefore simultaneously
a point of encounter

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:21.024
with life-threatening pathogens and toxins

00:06:21.024 --> 00:06:24.823
and the site of absorption
for life sustaining nutrients.

00:06:25.940 --> 00:06:29.450
Consequently, the intestine
is tasked with the challenge

00:06:29.450 --> 00:06:32.700
of balancing its primary
functions of nutrient uptake,

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:36.890
bringing things in, with host
defense, keeping things out,

00:06:36.890 --> 00:06:38.520
in response to a complex

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:40.793
and constantly changing environment.

00:06:41.630 --> 00:06:45.110
This challenge is particularly
daunting for omnivores

00:06:45.110 --> 00:06:49.010
like ourselves, whose diets
change on daily, seasonal,

00:06:49.010 --> 00:06:52.690
and lifelong timescales
alongside encounters

00:06:52.690 --> 00:06:56.010
with ingested toxins, enteric pathogens,

00:06:56.010 --> 00:06:58.673
and trillions of commensal microbes.

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:02.190
Omnivorous lifestyles therefore require

00:07:02.190 --> 00:07:04.270
that the GI tract be adaptable

00:07:04.270 --> 00:07:07.363
to the dynamic nature of our environments.

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:10.900
My doctoral studies examined the cellular

00:07:10.900 --> 00:07:14.063
and molecular mechanisms that
confer this adaptability.

00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:17.400
Using mice as a model system,

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:19.820
I discovered that the machinery involved

00:07:19.820 --> 00:07:22.470
in the digestion and
absorption of carbohydrates

00:07:22.470 --> 00:07:27.182
was induced on demand in
response to a high carb diet.

00:07:27.182 --> 00:07:30.380
This on-demand induction
involves changing the

00:07:30.380 --> 00:07:33.880
cellular composition of
the intestinal epithelium,

00:07:33.880 --> 00:07:35.790
demonstrating that the GI tract

00:07:35.790 --> 00:07:38.120
can undergo rapid tissue remodeling

00:07:38.120 --> 00:07:40.790
in response to different diets.

00:07:40.790 --> 00:07:44.760
And my most surprising discovery
was that a specific type

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:49.000
of immune cell called gamma
delta T cells was required

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:53.170
for this intestinal
adaptation to carbohydrates.

00:07:53.170 --> 00:07:55.930
This was the first demonstration
that an immune cell

00:07:55.930 --> 00:07:59.420
can directly regulate nutrient
uptake in the intestine

00:07:59.420 --> 00:08:02.690
and has established an
entirely new area of research

00:08:02.690 --> 00:08:06.053
pertaining to the connection
between nutrition and immunity.

00:08:07.330 --> 00:08:09.730
By linking nutrient
uptake and host defense

00:08:09.730 --> 00:08:12.160
at the cellular and molecular levels,

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:14.970
these adaptations conferred
by the immune system

00:08:14.970 --> 00:08:18.120
enable this complex tissue
to adjust the balance

00:08:18.120 --> 00:08:20.610
between nutrient uptake and host defense

00:08:20.610 --> 00:08:23.720
in response to environmental change.

00:08:23.720 --> 00:08:24.840
Thank you very much

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:27.643
and congratulations to
all of the graduates.

